@@ -0,0 +1,82 @@
+# AI boundary candidate review
+
+**Status:** implemented read-only review capability in the PR #227 branch. This is
+not an autonomous verification or release-readiness claim.
+
+## Candidate-ID interface
+
+Call the native Hunt capability using the running Hunt's advertised schema:
+
+```text
+POST /hunts/{hunt_id}/capabilities/ai.boundary.candidate.inspect
+```
+
+The request contains `idempotency_key` and `input.candidate_id` only. The server,
+not the caller, loads the candidate and evidence references. This uses the normal
+Hunt action lifecycle, durable accounting and receipts. It makes no target
+requests and consumes no verification slot or credential authority. A changed
+observation needs a new idempotency key; replaying an old key returns the old action.
+
+The result contains a `boundary-review-proposal/v1` with `needs_context` or
+`ready_for_review`, exact missing facts, available field names, context/policy
+digests, the observation ID and evidence-reference resolution. Review readiness
+is not proof, execution readiness, or a grant of authority. The output has no
+executable fragment and cannot be submitted as a materializable proposal.
+
+## Ownership and evidence
+
+The lookup requires both the Hunt's asset and a candidate observation belonging
+to that Hunt. A candidate's existence elsewhere is not disclosed. The latest
+observation is selected within that Hunt, not globally; a later sighting by a
+different Hunt cannot replace its context or evidence. All reads use a single
+read-only repeatable-read transaction.
+
+UUID references are resolved against this Hunt's action records, receipt links
+and asset-bound HTTP transaction metadata. Unknown, deleted, cross-Hunt and
+legacy textual references remain explicit gaps. Receipt linkage does not validate
+receipt contents or cryptographic proof. No raw request/response bodies, prompts,
+credentials or policy prose are returned. The lookup handles at most 100 references
+and reports overflow instead of silently truncating it.
+
+## Typed context persistence
+
+`locus.ai_boundary_context` preserves a bounded JSON object through candidate
+normalization and storage. Its explicit fields are optional owner/attacker identity
+bindings, existing boundary verifier/state/tool fields and an
+`invariant_contract_id`. Identity bindings are stored observations, not verified
+identities. State values are JSON scalars. Unknown fields and credential-shaped
+objects are rejected at request validation.
+
+The context is copied into each immutable candidate observation; it is excluded
+from the candidate fingerprint so changing review metadata does not change the
+identity of the observed surface. Ordinary metadata edits preserve the last
+snapshot belonging to the same Hunt, not the shared row's context.
+
+Earlier PR builds stringified this object. The reader does not evaluate or guess
+through legacy Python representations: those observations return a context gap.
+A fresh candidate observation supplies corrected typed context.
+
+## Policy and missing facts
+
+A policy reference must resolve to a structurally valid, approved invariant on the
+same target. A field merely claiming `expected_rule_source=operator` is not
+approval. Even a valid stored invariant requires human review of its applicability
+to the selected facts. Cross-tenant access is not automatically assumed forbidden.
+
+The lookup does not invent verifier endpoints from action routes, fill telemetry
+paths with defaults, synthesize prompts, decrypt credentials, provision fixtures,
+queue scans or promote findings. Missing principal bindings, independent verifiers,
+policy references and evidence are reported as specific gaps.
+
+## Integration boundary
+
+`ai.boundary.candidate.inspect` is registered in the native capability registry and
+implemented by the existing inline Hunt dispatcher. The draft compiler,
+materializer and verification REST routes elsewhere in PR #227 are separate. An
+Arsenal entry alone never made those routes callable as Hunt capabilities; the
+unwired PR-only entries have been removed rather than advertised as working tools.
+
+Tests cover typed persistence, per-Hunt observation ownership, target/plane
+separation, evidence resolution, policy references, legacy records, bounded input,
+no raw-data output, and the actual registered inline adapter branch. They do not
+claim live agent verification or production-agent coverage.
